Methods on generic structs
==========================

A generic struct may declare methods that reference its type
parameter. Each monomorphization gets its own method body
keyed by the concrete `T`; `self` typing flows through
unchanged.

    module sysl.tests.lang_features

    struct GsmBox[T]
        v: T

Basic getter method
-------------------

    GsmBox[T].get() -> T = self.v

    #test
    test_method_get_int() -> unit
        val b = GsmBox[int](42)
        assert(b.get() == 42, s"b.get() should be 42, got ${b.get()}")

    #test
    test_method_get_bool() -> unit
        val b = GsmBox[bool](true)
        assert(b.get(), "b.get() should be true")

Mutating method — `self` is `&self`
-----------------------------------

The method body assigns through `self.v`, which is shorthand
for `(&self).v`. The caller must observe the mutation;
otherwise the method ran on a copy.

    GsmBox[T].set(x: T) -> unit
        self.v = x

    #test
    test_method_set_int() -> unit
        var b = GsmBox[int](0)
        b.set(99)
        assert(b.v == 99, s"after set(99), b.v should be 99, got ${b.v}")

    #test
    test_method_set_then_get() -> unit
        var b = GsmBox[int](0)
        b.set(7)
        assert(b.get() == 7, s"after set(7), b.get() should be 7, got ${b.get()}")

Method on a two-parameter generic
---------------------------------

    struct GsmPair[A, B]
        a: A
        b: B

    GsmPair[A, B].swap() -> GsmPair[B, A] = GsmPair[B, A](self.b, self.a)

    #test
    test_method_swap() -> unit
        val p = GsmPair[int, bool](42, true)
        val q = p.swap()
        assert(q.a, "swapped.a should be true")
        assert(q.b == 42, s"swapped.b should be 42, got ${q.b}")

Chained method call
-------------------

    GsmBox[T].set_then_get(x: T) -> T
        self.set(x)
        return self.get()

    #test
    test_chained_method() -> unit
        var b = GsmBox[int](0)
        assert(b.set_then_get(123) == 123, s"chained should be 123, got ${b.set_then_get(0)}")
